{
  "identifier": "NG6491",
  "full_title": "Witches at their Incantations",
  "short_title": "Witches at their Incantations",
  "object_name": "Picture",
  "style": "baroque",
  "material": "oil on canvas",
  "dimensions": "72 × 132 cm",
  "production_date": "about 1646",
  "brief_description": "Beneath a pitch-dark sky, bizarre and terrifying magical spells are being cast. Monstrous figures, some of them naked, are arranged as if on a stage set, illuminated by scattered pools of light.\n\nIn the centre, one witch smokes the corpse of a criminal that hangs from a withered tree while her companion cuts its toenails. In front of them, naked witches use a wax effigy to cast a love spell; another, surrounded by a catalogue of magical aids, stirs a cauldron. On the extreme left, two men exhume a corpse and force it to sign a document while away to the right, a swaddled infant is held above the gaping jaws of a monster.\n\nThis macabre scene evokes a witches' sabbath, a gathering where the devil was worshipped. Salvator Rosa developed an interest in the supernatural while working in Florence in the 1640s and probably painted this remarkable picture around 1646.",
  "accession_date": "1984-01-01",
